1. Variable Coding & Group Definition
• Group Variable: SadOrHopeless
• Definition: Whether the student felt so sad or hopeless almost every day for two weeks or more in a row that they stopped doing some usual activities during the past 12 months.
• Original Coding: 1 = Yes; 2 = No.
• Recoding Decision: * Group 1 (Success): Original code 1 (Felt sad/hopeless).
• Group 0 (Failure): Original code 2 (Did not feel sad/hopeless).
• Data Cleaning: All invalid codes or blank entries were treated as missing values (NaN) and excluded from the calculation.
2.Response Variable: CurrentCigaretteUse
• Definition: The number of days the student smoked cigarettes during the past 30 days.
• Original Coding: 1 = 0 days; 2 = 1 or 2 days; 3 = 3 to 5 days; 4 = 6 to 9 days; 5 = 10 to 19 days; 6 = 20 to 29 days; 7 = All 30 days.
• Recoding Decision (Binary):
• Success (1): Original codes 2 through 7 (Currently uses cigarettes).
• Failure (0): Original code 1 (Does not currently use cigarettes).
• Data Cleaning: Non-numeric or blank responses were excluded to ensure the reliability of the proportion estimate.
3. Method Choice
• Selected Method: Two-Proportion Z-Test.
• Reasoning: * The research question compares two independent groups (Sad vs. Not Sad).
• The response variable is categorical/binary (Uses cigarettes: Yes/No) after recoding.
• Therefore, a test of proportions is required rather than a t-test (which is for quantitative means).
4. Assumptions Considered
To ensure the validity of the two-sample inference, the following assumptions were verified:
	1.Independence: The samples are assumed to be independent, as students were surveyed individually across different schools.
  2.Randomization: The YRBS 2007 dataset uses a multi-stage cluster sampling design, which we treat as a representative random sample of the U.S. high school population.
  3.Large Counts Condition: Both groups have more than 10 "successes" (smokers) and 10 "failures" (non-smokers).
• Group 1 (Sad): n_1 \hat{p}_1 \ge 10 and n_1(1-\hat{p}_1) \ge 10
• Group 0 (Not Sad): n_0 \hat{p}_0 \ge 10 and n_0(1-\hat{p}_0) \ge 10
• This justifies the use of the Normal approximation for the Z-test.
